<article class="env-lemma" id="0EVE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EVE">Lemma <span data-tag="0EVE">38.37.8</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a sheaf on one of the sites $(\mathit{Sch}/S)_ h$ constructed in Definition <a href="/tag/0EU1" data-tag="0EU1">38.34.13</a>. Let $X \to X'$ be a morphism of $(\mathit{Sch}/S)_ h$ which is a thickening and of finite presentation. Then $\mathcal{F}(X') \to \mathcal{F}(X)$ is bijective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      First proof. Observe that $X \to X'$ is a proper surjective morphism of finite presentation and $X \times _{X'} X = X$. By the sheaf property for the h covering $\{ X \to X'\} $ (Lemma <a href="/tag/0ETW" data-tag="0ETW">38.34.7</a>) we conclude. 
    <p>
      Second proof (silly). The blow up of $X'$ in $X$ is the empty scheme. The reason is that the affine blowup algebra $A[\frac{I}{a}]$ (Algebra, Section <a href="/tag/052P" data-tag="052P">10.70</a>) is zero if $a$ is a nilpotent element of $A$. Details omitted. Hence we get an almost blow up square of the form 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ \emptyset \ar[r] \ar[d] &  \emptyset \ar[d] \\  X \ar[r] &  X' }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Since $\mathcal{F}$ is a sheaf we have that $\mathcal{F}(\emptyset )$ is a singleton. Applying Lemma <a href="/tag/0EVD" data-tag="0EVD">38.37.7</a> we get the conclusion. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>